# P7a pre-publish round 3 (review C1/C2/M3): THIS FILE PINS NO REGISTRY AND NO TOKEN.
#
# It used to carry `@yanlinglabs:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com` as a "safe default", and that
# line is exactly what made the npm leg unreachable. pnpm's `pickRegistryForPackage` is
# `(scope && registries[scope]) ?? registries.default`, and `--registry` sets only
# `registries.default` -- so a scope pin here beats the CLI flag, in `pnpm publish` and in
# `pnpm view` alike (measured). pnpm's `copyNpmrc` then carries the same file into the directory npm
# runs in, so the pin wins a second time at the npm layer.
#
# WHO ACTUALLY NEEDS A SCOPE PIN, and it is nobody this file serves:
#   * the WORKSPACE does not: every `@yanlinglabs/*` dependency is `workspace:*`, resolved from the
#     monorepo, never fetched;
#   * a CONSUMER does, and sets it in THEIR OWN `.npmrc` -- the package READMEs give them the line;
#   * each PUBLISH JOB does, and gets it from `actions/setup-node` (`registry-url` + `scope`), which
#     writes a throwaway userconfig with the matching `_authToken` and points `NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG`
#     at it. One mechanism chooses the registry AND the credential together, per job, which is the
#     property this file could never have: a committed pin is global, and there are two registries.
#
# A `${NODE_AUTH_TOKEN}` placeholder is gone with it. It authenticated nothing locally and made every
# pnpm invocation in the repo print `WARN Issue while reading ".npmrc". Failed to replace env in
# config` (review M3).
#
# The file is kept (rather than deleted) so this reasoning has somewhere to live: the next person to
# reach for a scope pin should read why it was removed before adding it back.
